} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "4 –  8 August 1992"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/674"} "Canal Olímpico de Cataluña, Castelldefels"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/255967"} "54 from 27 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "Germany’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/9894"} "Kay Bluhm"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/9915"} "Torsten Gutsche"] " had been even more dominant in the K-2 1000 at the World Championships than they had been in the K-2 500 and were the overwhelming favorites to take the title at the 1992 Summer Olympics, even with the presence of defending Olympic champions " [:a {:href "/athletes/11057"} "Greg Barton"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/11059"} "Norman Bellingham"] " of the United States. The Germans dominated the opening round, winning their heat in Olympic record time over six seconds ahead of " [:a {:href "/athletes/10620"} "Florintin Tataru"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/10607"} "Alexandru Popa"] ", the victors in the other heat, and over eight seconds ahead of anyone in their own. They received a greater challenge in the semi-finals, however, and bested the Americans in their heat by only 0.29 seconds, while " [:a {:href ""} "Gunnar Olsson"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/10805"} "Karl Sundqvist"] " of Sweden actually posted a faster time in their own heat. The Germans mustered their strength for the finals, however, and dispatched the Swedes easily to take gold, making them double Olympic champions due to their earlier victory in the K-2 500. Poland’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/10494"} "Grzegorz Kotowicz"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/10467"} "Dariusz Białkowski"] ", meanwhile, stole bronze from the Americans by a margin of 0.4 seconds."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Pair"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "Rep"] [:th {} "SF"] [:th {} "Final"